{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 6, :comments ["<b>Do not dishevel</b> — Do not let your hair grow long. From here [we learn] that a mourner is forbidden to cut his hair. \"But you — do not mix up the joy of the Makom.\" \"And you shall not die\" — the inference: if you do so, you will die. This is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Leviticus 10:6). But this statement of his, that from here we learn that a mourner is forbidden to cut his hair, is merely an asmakhta. Furthermore, according to this reading, the inference \"if you do [so] you will die\" would not be derived; rather, it would be a matter of permission for the sons of Aaron, as though He said: You are not obligated to let your hair grow long and to rend your garments like other mourners, \"and you shall not die\" — that you will not be liable to death for this, whereas other mourners are liable if they do not do so. And thus they said in the Gemara in the chapter HaMashkin (Mo'ed Katan 24a): A mourner who did not let his hair grow long and did not rend is liable to death, as it is said, \"Do not let your hair grow long and do not rend your garments and you shall not die\" — the inference: another [mourner] who did not let his hair grow long and did not rend is liable to death. And all of this is in the manner of asmakhtot in the Talmud. And the obligations of peri'ah (letting the hair grow) and perima (rending) in mourning are [only] from Rabbinic law, or [in the case of] the first day [of mourning] perhaps a Halakha le-Moshe mi-Sinai, derived from the principle of oninut according to the view of the Geonim. But the primary meaning of this verse is an admonition to the sons of Aaron.\n\nNow hear its explanation: Our Rabbis have already stated (Horayot 12b) that a Kohen Gadol (High Priest) may offer sacrifices while he is an onen (mourner before burial) and may not eat [of them], and if his dead relative has not yet been buried, he is forbidden to let his hair grow long and to rend on his account and to become impure for him, as it is said (Leviticus 21:10–11), \"He shall not let his hair grow long and he shall not rend his garments, and for any dead soul he shall not come.\" And because he is permitted to offer sacrifices while he is an onen, Scripture said of him, \"And from the Sanctuary he shall not go out and he shall not profane the Sanctuary of his Elohim\" (Leviticus 21:12). And the reason He admonished him not to leave the Sanctuary and abandon the service is that this constitutes a profanation of the Sanctuary.\n\nNow this admonition not to leave the Sanctuary during the service applies to all the priests, but it teaches regarding the Kohen Gadol that he may offer sacrifices while an onen. Since this is so, if he were to abandon the service and go out in his state of aninut, he would be liable just like any priest who abandons the service and goes out of his own volition. Now Moses admonished Aaron here that he must not let his hair grow long and must not rend, for this is a commandment that applies to him for all generations, since he is the Kohen Gadol. But his sons, who were [in the category of] ordinary priests, were not permitted to offer sacrifices on this day because they were onenin, and an onen who performs service has profaned [it], and they were obligated to become impure for their brothers. And likewise, they were permitted — or rather obligated — to let their hair grow long and to rend, and Moses admonished them against all of this as a hora'at sha'ah (a provisional decree for the moment).\n\nTherefore he commanded Mishael and Elzaphan to become impure for the dead — they and not the priests — and he commanded [Aaron and his remaining sons] that they should not let their hair grow long and should not rend like their father, and that they should not weep at all. And this is the meaning of \"And your brothers, the whole house of Israel, shall bewail the burning\" — and not you. And all of this was in order not to mix up the joy of the Makom, and He was stringent in this, making them liable to death if they mixed up the joy at all.\n\nAnd it is possible that Moses was commanded in this by the Gevurah, even though it was not written, or that he derived it from what they were previously commanded (Leviticus 8:35), \"And you shall keep the charge of HaShem and you shall not die,\" for \"the charge of HaShem\" includes also the eighth day. And it was revealed and known before Him who spoke and the world came into being that the sons of Aaron were destined to be onenin, yet He commanded that they must not leave the Sanctuary. If so, they must not become impure and must not mourn at all.\n\nNow the sons of Aaron, the anointed priests — even though they were ordinary priests — during the days of milu'im (consecration) their law was treated like the law of the meshuach milchamah (anointed priest for war) for all generations: he does not let his hair grow long, does not rend, and does not become impure for relatives, like the Kohen Gadol (Horayot 3b), but he does not offer sacrifices while an onen. And they too did not offer [sacrifices] on that day, as they derived (Zevachim 101a): \"'Behold, this day they have offered their sin-offering' — did they offer, given that they were ordinary priests? It was I [Aaron] who brought near, for I am the Kohen Gadol.\"\n\nNow in this entire incident, nothing was nullified in the milu'im, for the offerings had already been brought, and by evening they ate all of them that was fit for eating. And it is possible that this [status] lasted all the days of Elazar and Itamar, that their law was like that of the meshuach milchamah, because they were anointed with the anointing oil. And this would be the meaning of \"for the anointing oil of HaShem is upon you.\"\n\nAnd I have already mentioned (on Leviticus 8:35) the baraita of Torat Kohanim (at the beginning of parashat Shemini, sec. 42) which states: \"'And from the entrance of the Tent of Meeting you shall not go out' — I might think [this applies] during the service but not outside the service? Scripture says, 'And from the Sanctuary he shall not go out and he shall not profane' (Leviticus 21:12). When does he not go out and not profane? You must say: during the service. 'Lest you die' — from the implication of the negative you hear the positive [inference]. 'For the anointing oil of HaShem is upon you' — what does this come to teach? For I might say: I have only Aaron and his sons, who were anointed with the anointing oil, who are liable to death if they go out during the service. From where do I derive all the priests in all generations? Scripture says, 'For the anointing oil of HaShem is upon you.'\"\n\nAnd I have also found in the Tosefta of Sanhedrin (chapter 4, halakha 1): They said to Rabbi Yehudah: \"'From the Sanctuary he shall not go out\" applies only during the service alone. Now according to this, this admonition and this punishment applied to Aaron and to his sons: if they went out from the entrance of the Tent of Meeting during the service, they would be liable. And Aaron was offering sacrifices on this day, and [Moses] admonished him not to abandon the service and go out. But his sons on this day were onenin and were not performing service — how could they be included in this? Perhaps we must say that Moses said to all of them: \"And from the entrance of the Tent of Meeting you shall not go out during the service, lest you die\" — and they knew that Aaron may offer sacrifices while an onen, and he was under this admonition from now on; and his sons were included in the admonition and the punishment once they would become fit for service, from this day onward.\n\nFor this reason he said here, in an unspecified manner, \"And from the entrance of the Tent of Meeting you shall not go out,\" and did not specify until when — whether until evening or the next day — for the intent is that they must not abandon the service, and this is a commandment that applies for all generations. And it is possible that for the sons there was a hora'at sha'ah that they should not go out from there on this day like their father, for the honor of the joy; but the priests in all generations are admonished not to abandon service, from the verse that says \"And from the Sanctuary he shall not go out and he shall not profane,\" for any priest who goes out and abandons his service profanes [the Sanctuary]. And they needed to learn from here only the punishment, which was extended so that anyone who abandons the service and goes out is [liable to] death, from \"for the anointing oil of HaShem is upon you\" — for the anointing applies to them for all generations, as it is said (Exodus 40:15), \"And it shall be for them as their anointing for an everlasting priesthood throughout their generations.\"\n\nAnd thus they taught there in the baraita of Torat Kohanim (at the beginning of the parashah, sec. 28): \"From here they said: Those executed by the court are not given any attention for anything. Lest you say: Behold, we are going to our homes to mourn, to be onenin and to weep — but has not the Makom already charged us: 'You shall not go out from the Sanctuary.'\"\n\nIt appears from here that there was a hora'at sha'ah that those who died were regarded as though executed by a heavenly court for transgressions, who come and inquire after the well-being of the judges and after the well-being of the witnesses (Sanhedrin 46b). So too here [Nadab and Abihu] stand before HaShem on the day of His wedding and on the day of the gladness of His heart: \"On the day of His wedding\" — this is Sinai; \"and on the day of the gladness of His heart\" — this is the joy of the Tent of Meeting (Numbers Rabbah 12:8)."], :book :leviticus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 10, :notes {0 "The cryptic final paragraph parallels the talmudic passage (Sanhedrin 46b) about those executed by a human court, drawing an analogy to Nadab and Abihu as executed by a heavenly court; Ramban's closing midrashic citations are rendered literally without further expansion."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:52:57.135226445Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}